{"version": 2, "width": 110, "height": 24, "timestamp": 1794530226, "title": "learn/bayes \u00b7 not-ambitious run 1", "env": {"TERM": "xterm-256color"}, "theme": {"fg": "#d8dee9", "bg": "#1e1f1c", "palette": "#272822:#f92672:#a6e22e:#f4bf75:#66d9ef:#ae81ff:#a1efe4:#f8f8f2:#75715e:#f92672:#a6e22e:#f4bf75:#66d9ef:#ae81ff:#a1efe4:#f9f8f5"}}
[0.0, "o", "\u001b[?25l"]
[0.0, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ Explain Bayes' theorem with an interactive visualisation, as a single web page\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[44.679, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mWrote 1 file\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Write(<workspace>/bayes.html)\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[49.502, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ I've written the page to `<workspace>/bayes.html`. Open it in any browser. It's a single self-contained file\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  with no dependencies. I haven't opened it in a browser, so the layout and behaviour are untested.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What's on the page**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  1. **Scenario controls.** Three sliders set the prior P(H), the hit rate P(E|H), and the false-alarm rate\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  P(E|¬H). Four presets load worked examples: a rare disease test, a spam filter, a very accurate test for a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  very rare condition, and a two-headed coin. The posterior P(H|E) is shown large, with a bar that marks prior\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  against posterior.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  2. **1,000-people dot grid.** This shows the same calculation as counts. True hits are blue and false alarms\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  are orange. In the default scenario (1% prevalence, 90% hit rate, 9% false alarms), about 9 true hits sit\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  among about 98 false alarms, so a positive result means only about 8% chance of disease.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  3. **Area diagram.** Width is the prior and height is the likelihood. The posterior is the blue area divided\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  by blue plus orange.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  4. **The formula with your numbers.** It shows the theorem, then your values substituted in, then a step-by-\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  step breakdown. A short note explains the base-rate fallacy.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  The page follows your system's light or dark setting and works on narrow screens.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  One thing to check when you open it: the small \"E rows\" label beside the area diagram is my own addition. It\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  repeats the posterior percentage and may be redundant, so cut it if it looks cluttered.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[53.941, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ Push it further.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[101.845, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mRan 1 shell command\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Bash(cd \"<workspace>\" && python3 -I - <<'EOF')\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[108.615, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ I've extended `bayes.html` with five new sections. It parses without syntax errors, but I haven't opened it\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  in a browser, so the layout and interactions are untested.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Draggable area diagram (section 3).** You can now drag the diagram itself.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - The vertical divider sets the prior.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - Dragging up or down in the left column sets the hit rate.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - Dragging up or down in the right column sets the false-alarm rate.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - Everything else on the page updates live while you drag.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Sequential updating (section 5).** \"Observe E\" and \"Observe no E\" feed each posterior back in as the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  next prior, so you can watch belief build up or fall back. A row of chips records the path. This assumes\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  each observation is independent given H.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Odds form (section 6).** This shows prior odds × likelihood ratio = posterior odds, with a plain-\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  language note on whether the evidence supports H, counts against it, or tells you nothing.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Posterior vs. prior curve (section 7).** It plots the posterior against every possible prior, with a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  dashed \"no update\" diagonal and your current point marked. This makes it clear why a rare hypothesis stays\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  unlikely even after a positive result.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Simulation (section 8).** Buttons add 100 or 1,000 random people drawn from your probabilities. It keeps\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  only those showing the evidence and tallies how many really are H, with a bar that converges on the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  theoretical posterior. It resets whenever you change a parameter.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I also changed the prior handling so chained updates carry the exact value rather than a value rounded to\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the slider's step. Moving a slider, dragging the diagram, or choosing a preset clears the chain.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
